# Claude Agent SDK leverage

The Flynn/SQLite migration is governed by ADR-0012 and AGENTS.md. This native-SDK
preference applies to the existing Claude runtime, not to its replacement. Flynn owns
generic execution durability; VFX retains domain authority, replay and acceptance.
Breaking APIs and explicit schema refusal are permitted; no compatibility layer is owed.

Treat the Claude Agent SDK as a production runtime, not merely prompt transport. Inspect the
installed SDK before building custom orchestration and prefer its strongest applicable native
mechanism when that improves control, observability, or agent performance.

- Prefer native typed messages, tools, hooks, permissions and tool policy, MCP integration,
  lifecycle, cancellation, resume, model configuration, and usage reporting over duplicate glue.
- Preserve structured events, errors, termination reasons, usage, and results until their owning
  boundary records them; do not flatten authority into prose prematurely.
- Give roles bounded tools and compiled active-unit context. SDK capability does not imply broad
  context, unrestricted mutation, or expanded authority.
- Use hooks and tool policy for mechanical enforcement; prompts do not replace enforceable gates.
- Resume only when checkpoint, journal, authority generation, and active-unit identity match.
- Select model, effort, turns, tokens, and cost from role needs and measured uncertainty. Do not
  maximize every setting. Parallelize evidence, but serialize authoritative mutation/publication.
- Verify the installed SDK lacks a needed primitive before adding a workaround. Pin and test every
  relied-on behavior; fail closed on incompatible schema, hook, tool, permission, or session changes.
- Adopt features only for measured capability or control gains. SDK features never replace domain
  contracts, replay, deterministic validation, or acceptance evidence.
